pairtime install

This is everything pairtime install changes on this computer. Nothing
changes until you say yes, and pairtime uninstall takes it all back out.

pairtime goes in /home/teen/.local/bin/pairtime.
It runs in local-only mode: what it records stays on this computer.

AI tools pairtime looked for:
  Claude Code         found at /home/teen/.claude
  Cursor              not found
  GitHub Copilot CLI  not found
  OpenAI Codex        not found
  Gemini CLI          not found

[x] 1. Start pairtime when you log in

    pairtime's agent starts in the background each time you log in, so it
    notices when you build without you starting it.

    Saying no doesn't stop you from using pairtime.
    Without it, pairtime starts again after you log in only when a hook
    starts it or you run pairtime agent, so it misses what comes before.
    You can turn it off any time in your desktop's Startup Applications, or
    with pairtime uninstall.

    --- /dev/null
    +++ /home/teen/.config/autostart/pairtime.desktop
    @@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
    +[Desktop Entry]
    +Type=Application
    +Name=pairtime
    +Comment=Counts the time you spend building with AI
    +Exec=/home/teen/.local/bin/pairtime agent
    +TryExec=/home/teen/.local/bin/pairtime
    +Terminal=false

[x] 2. Show your time as "calibrating" until pairtime's scoring is frozen

    You volunteered to help calibrate pairtime, so until its scoring reaches
    version 1.0.0, when its rules are frozen, pairtime today, recap,
    statusline and the local dashboard show your time as "calibrating". They
    keep the agent's time, your moments and the loops pairtime caught, and
    leave out everything your time could be worked out from, so nobody
    learns to steer the rules while they are being calibrated. pairtime
    still counts all of your time, and its files on this computer keep every
    number.

    Saying no doesn't stop you from using pairtime.
    Without it, pairtime shows your time as its rules count it now, while
    they are being calibrated.
    You can turn it off any time with shadow = false under [display] in
    config.toml. It turns off by itself once pairtime's scoring reaches
    1.0.0.

    --- /home/teen/.config/pairtime/config.toml
    +++ /home/teen/.config/pairtime/config.toml
    @@ -2,4 +2,4 @@
     
     [display]
     # ***
    -shadow = false
    +shadow = true

[x] 3. Add pairtime's hooks to Claude Code

    Claude Code runs pairtime hook when a session starts or ends, when you
    send a prompt, and when its agent stops, asks you something, edits a
    file, runs a command or starts a subagent, so pairtime knows when you
    prompt and when the agent works. pairtime's hooks go after the hooks
    already there, which stay as they are.
    Each hook call adds a moment to Claude Code's turn: a test call just now
    took 12 ms.

    Saying no doesn't stop you from using pairtime.
    Without them, pairtime doesn't see your Claude Code prompts, and time it
    can't see isn't credited.
    You can take them out any time with pairtime uninstall.

    --- /dev/null
